// Commercial ammunition catalog — ammo-only data (never sight height, zero,
// or conditions; those belong to the shooter's own setup, not the ammo).
// Selecting an entry in the UI fills only muzzleVelocity/ballisticCoefficient/
// dragModel/grains, leaving everything else on the form untouched.
//
// dragModel is "G1" for almost every entry in this file — most of these
// manufacturers only publish G1 in the BULK product listings this catalog
// was built from, and G1 is what's available at catalog scale. Documented
// exceptions that carry "G7" and the G7 BC: the 46 Hornady ELD Match/ELD-X
// entries (Hornady's own G7 from hornady.com/bc), and every Berger entry
// (Berger publishes a radar-measured G7 for all its bullets — see the
// Berger section below). Everything else on G1 stays a real accuracy gap
// for modern boat-tail bullets (where G7
// tracks drag more faithfully), documented here rather than hidden.
//
// bcSource on every entry records where its BC actually came from:
//   "published"        — manufacturer's own stated BC, used as-is. For 46
//                          Hornady ELD Match/ELD-X entries specifically,
//                          this is Hornady's own G7 BC from their dedicated
//                          technical page (hornady.com/bc, not their bulk
//                          product listing) — see scripts/applyHornadyBC.mjs
//                          and hornady_bc_raw.mjs for the source data and
//                          how it was matched into this catalog.
//   "derived-hornady"   — Hornady publishes no BC at all in its bulk chart, so
//                          BC was back-calculated (bisection against this
//                          app's own solver/drag-table physics, in
//                          scripts/deriveBC.mjs) from Hornady's own published
//                          muzzle velocity + farthest-range velocity. Verified
//                          against a case with 5 published points (not just
//                          the fitted one): RMS residual 0.25 fps.
//   "derived-federal"   — same technique for the majority of Federal's rifle
//                          line that doesn't publish BC (only Gold Medal and
//                          a handful of others do).
//   "derived-nosler"    — same technique for every Nosler entry (Nosler
//                          publishes muzzle + downrange velocities but no BC).
//                          Fit at 500 yd, checked against Nosler's 300/400/600
//                          yd figures; spot-checks land within ~1% of Nosler's
//                          own published bullet BCs (28 Nosler 175 ABLR:
//                          derived 0.654 vs published 0.648). ~10 loads whose
//                          published tables were internally inconsistent or
//                          implied a physically impossible BC were dropped —
//                          see scripts/buildNosler.mjs.
// Derived entries are a legitimate inverse-problem fit against a
// manufacturer's own real-world data — not circular validation against this
// app's own trajectories, and not a fabricated placeholder number.
//
// ---- Remington ----
// Source: Remington 2026 Ammunition Catalog, "Ballistics" section (rifle
// table), pages 57-70. https://www.remington.com/on/demandware.static/-/Library-Sites-RemingtonSharedLibrary/default/v5ac6c017161b18362fea8ab6e7ab4ac6a5fbd9cb/contentDocuments/catalog/2026-Catalog-Web.pdf
// Pulled 2026-08-26. BC is published (bulk table shows G1 only — confirmed by
// cross-checking the 30-06/172gr Speer Impact row: catalog shows 0.522,
// matching Remington's own individually-published G1 figure exactly, not the
// 0.265 G7 figure also on that product page). A few rows had no published
// velocity data at all (all "--") and were omitted: 7mm PRC/R7MMPRC,
// 300 PRC/RT300PRC, 300 AAC Blackout/R300AAC6.
//
// ---- Weatherby ----
// Source: weatherby.com live ballistics-charts page (server-rendered HTML
// table), pulled 2026-08-26. BC is published directly (G1) for every entry —
// the cleanest of the four sources.
//
// ---- Hornady ----
// Source: Hornady's 2022 Standard Ballistics Chart PDF (rifle + Frontier
// Cartridge sections), user-supplied link, pulled 2026-08-26. Hornady
// publishes muzzle velocity and downrange velocities but never BC, so every
// Hornady entry's BC is derived (see above). Fit conditions: 59F, 29.92 inHg
// (Hornady doesn't state its own test atmosphere, so this app's standard
// default was used).
//
// ---- Federal ----
// Source: Federal's 2019 Ballistics chart PDF (all rifle product lines —
// Big Game, Cape-Shok, Varmint & Predator, Gold Medal, Fusion, Fusion MSR,
// Power-Shok, American Eagle, Non-Typical), pulled 2026-08-26. Federal
// states its own test conditions explicitly (59F, 29.53 inHg, sea level) —
// used for every derived Federal entry. BC is published directly only for
// Gold Medal and a scattering of others (~40 of 335 entries); the rest are
// derived (see above).
//
// ---- Winchester ----
// Source: the public JSON API behind Winchester's own ballistics calculator
// (ballisticscalculator.winchester.com -> wdm2.azurewebsites.net/api/bc/v3),
// pulled 2026-09-10 — see scripts/winchester_raw.mjs (harvest) and
// scripts/buildWinchester.mjs (transform + cartridge-name normalization).
// Every Winchester entry's BC is published (G1). 8 loads were dropped: 2 with
// no muzzle velocity, 6 (mostly the budget "Max Point" line) with no
// published BC and no reliable way to derive one — Winchester's own
// Trajectory endpoint is broken for exactly those BC-less loads, and no
// other first-party source states a BC for them.
//
// ---- Barnes ----
// Source: Barnes's "Centerfire Rifle Ballistics" master chart PDF
// (barnesbullets.com/content/Barnes-Ballistics-Charts/CFR-Imperial.pdf),
// pulled 2026-09-10. Every entry's BC is published (G1 — Barnes prints the
// G1 BC on the box). All product lines: VOR-TX, VOR-TX LR / RR / Safari,
// Precision Match, Harvest, Pioneer (lever), Suppressor Series, Euro. The
// chart is laid out as separate per-column text frames so `pdftotext`
// mangles it — extracted with PyMuPDF's table detection instead (see
// scripts/barnes_raw.py + scripts/buildBarnes.mjs). No drops.
//
// ---- Berger ----
// Source: the WooCommerce Store API behind bergerbullets.com
// (/wp-json/wc/store/products, category "Rifle Ammunition"), pulled
// 2026-09-10 — see scripts/berger_raw.mjs + scripts/buildBerger.mjs. Berger
// publishes a Doppler-radar-measured G7 BC (averaged 3000->1500 fps) for
// every load, so these 34 entries are the catalog's cleanest G7 data:
// dragModel "G7", the G7 value, bcSource "published". 4 Classic Hunter
// loads Berger states no muzzle velocity for were dropped.
//
// ---- Nosler ----
// Source: nosler.com (Magento), pulled 2026-09-10 — the ammo product-line
// category pages for URLs, then each product page's "More Information" and
// "VELOCITY (FPS)" tables. See scripts/nosler_raw.mjs + scripts/buildNosler.mjs.
// Nosler publishes no BC anywhere, so every entry's G1 BC is derived
// (bcSource "derived-nosler" — see the bcSource notes above). ~29 loads were
// dropped: NoslerCustom loads with no ballistics table, two subsonic loads,
// and ~10 whose published tables were internally inconsistent or so flat
// they fit to a physically impossible BC (several big-bore tables).

